# STEP — the header.
#
# Declarations only; the implementation is
# `crates/runtime/src/drivers/step.rs`.
#
# ISO 10303-21, the exchange format every CAD system reads. A part
# leaves this kernel as STEP text and arrives in another system as the
# same solid — which is the only reason to have a format at all.
#
# WHY THIS IS A DRIVER AND THE TESSELLATOR IS NOT
# -----------------------------------------------
# STEP is TEXT. `CARTESIAN_POINT('',(0.,0.,0.))` is characters, entity
# references are `#12`, and parsing one means tokenising and resolving
# a reference graph. None of that is geometry, and the language has
# integers rather than strings.
#
# Tessellation went the other way — into `.lang` — because it decides
# geometry, and geometry the kernel does not own is geometry the kernel
# cannot guarantee. The line is what the work IS, not what is easier.
#
# BYTES, NOT A FILE
# -----------------
# `serialize` returns the file's bytes and writes nothing. Whoever
# called it decides where they go: a test writes them beside itself, a
# server sends them, an addon hands them to JavaScript. A driver that
# opened files would make every caller inherit a path.
#
# One i32 per byte. A STEP file is ASCII, the list is short-lived, and
# packing four to a word would need shifts the language has no
# operators for.

from 'linen:brep' use Body

# The bytes of a STEP file describing this body.
driver fn serialize(body Body) List<i32>

# A body read back from STEP bytes.
#
# The other half, and the one that proves the first: a body that
# survives a round trip is a body the format actually carried. Anything
# unreadable comes back as a body with nothing in it, so a caller checks
# by counting rather than by catching.
driver fn parse(bytes List<i32>) Body

# A fixed piece of STEP text, by number — for tests that need to hand
# the reader something specific.
#
# There is no string literal in the language, so the fragments a test
# needs are named rather than written. Scaffolding, like `linen:bytes`:
# it goes away when text lands.
driver fn bytes_of(text i32) List<i32>
